WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.520 --> 00:00:04.060
There aren't many heroes in The Devil All
the Time, but if you look hard, you'll find

00:00:04.060 --> 00:00:04.860
at least one.

00:00:05.140 --> 00:00:08.700
Young Arvin Russell, played by Tom Holland,
is everything that the other residents of

00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:11.780
Coal Creek, West Virginia and Knockemstiff,
Ohio are not.

00:00:12.100 --> 00:00:15.880
He's kind and brave, always ready to stick
up for others, and never backs down from a

00:00:15.880 --> 00:00:16.380
fight.

00:00:16.700 --> 00:00:19.480
In other words, he's the perfect character
for Holland to play.

00:00:19.840 --> 00:00:21.560
Just look at Holland's first scene in the
movie.

00:00:21.860 --> 00:00:25.860
Eight years after a tragic and bizarre set
of circumstances left Arvin an orphan and

00:00:25.860 --> 00:00:29.930
forced him to move in with his grandmother
Emma, his great-uncle Erskell, and his stepsister

00:00:29.930 --> 00:00:32.060
Lenora, Arvin celebrates his birthday.

00:00:32.360 --> 00:00:36.191
On the surface, it's a sweet scene, but like
everything in The Devil All the Time, there's

00:00:36.200 --> 00:00:38.260
much more lurking just under the surface.

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:42.600
Not only is this sequence full of grim portents
about the Russell family's future, but it's

00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:46.120
the first time we see Arvin fully grown and
get a glimpse of the man he's become.

00:00:46.450 --> 00:00:49.440
Holland's performance in this scene tells
the audience everything they need to know

00:00:49.450 --> 00:00:54.040
about Arvin, plays on Holland's natural strengths,
and shows exactly why he's one of Hollywood's

00:00:54.040 --> 00:00:55.160
go-to leading men.

00:00:56.190 --> 00:01:00.110
Despite all the amoral, violent, and downright
evil characters that come and go throughout

00:01:00.110 --> 00:01:03.850
The Devil All the Time, you can't help but
get the sense that Arvin is a character who's

00:01:03.850 --> 00:01:05.200
deeply, inherently kind.

00:01:05.660 --> 00:01:07.000
This scene proves it from the start.

00:01:07.480 --> 00:01:09.140
Look at the party his family throws for him.

00:01:09.240 --> 00:01:11.399
There's not much to the cake that Emma bakes.

00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:12.789
It's lumpy and unfrosted.

00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:14.920
It doesn't even have real birthday candles.

00:01:15.020 --> 00:01:19.359
Arvin's sole present is a hand-me-down from
a father that Arvin had a complicated relationship

00:01:19.360 --> 00:01:23.200
with, a relic of a brutal war, and a very
odd gift for a teenager.

00:01:23.420 --> 00:01:24.700
But Arvin doesn't complain.

00:01:25.040 --> 00:01:26.480
In fact, he welcomes all of it.

00:01:26.940 --> 00:01:30.040
When he sees the cake, as modest as it is,
he smiles.

00:01:30.080 --> 00:01:34.289
Before blowing out his lone candle, he gives
his wish real thought, proving that his traumatic

00:01:34.289 --> 00:01:36.860
childhood hasn't robbed him of all his innocence.

00:01:37.060 --> 00:01:40.170
Despite the baggage attached to the gun, he
seems genuinely thankful.

00:01:41.340 --> 00:01:42.659
"It's the best present I ever got.

00:01:42.660 --> 00:01:43.360
Thank you, Uncle Erskell."

00:01:43.780 --> 00:01:48.360
And when Lenora leans in for a hug, Arvin
welcomes it, marking a stark contrast to 9-year-old

00:01:48.360 --> 00:01:50.960
Arvin's passive embrace of Emma in the scene
before.

00:01:51.400 --> 00:01:55.900
Arvin's later actions go even further in proving
his genuine nature, but this scene sets the

00:01:55.900 --> 00:01:56.400
stage.

00:01:56.860 --> 00:02:00.439
All throughout The Devil All the Time after
this moment, you never have any doubt that

00:02:00.440 --> 00:02:02.880
Arvin is a good person, no matter what he
does.

00:02:03.100 --> 00:02:05.200
That's the power Holland brings to the character.

00:02:06.100 --> 00:02:09.420
Young Arvin, played by Michael Banks Repeta,
is mostly reactive.

00:02:09.720 --> 00:02:12.300
Things happen to him, but he doesn't drive
the action.

00:02:12.460 --> 00:02:14.020
After the birthday party, that changes.

00:02:14.480 --> 00:02:18.460
Lenora is sweet but naive, and Emma and Erskell
aren't getting any younger.

00:02:18.460 --> 00:02:22.100
When Arvin blows out that candle, he effectively
becomes the man of the house.

00:02:22.550 --> 00:02:26.340
His family is now under his protection, and
he makes good on that dynamic as the film

00:02:26.340 --> 00:02:27.040
progresses.

00:02:27.340 --> 00:02:29.300
"I know you'd probably rather be doing something
else.

00:02:31.980 --> 00:02:34.540
We're kin, and all we got for each other."

00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:36.060
He knows it, too.

00:02:36.420 --> 00:02:40.300
Note the studied, respectful glance he gives
his father's gun when he receives it.

00:02:40.300 --> 00:02:43.450
This isn't a child who's ignoring the responsibilities
he's been given.

00:02:43.450 --> 00:02:46.900
This is a young man who's just been given
the power to kill, and he's going to use it

00:02:46.900 --> 00:02:47.400
wisely.

00:02:55.460 --> 00:02:57.260
This plays out as the film continues.

00:02:57.500 --> 00:02:59.420
It's Arvin who protects Lenora from bullies.

00:02:59.540 --> 00:03:02.760
It's Arvin who consoles Emma when Reverend
Teagardin humiliates her.

00:03:03.060 --> 00:03:06.860
And when things get really bad, it's Arvin
who sacrifices his happiness for the sake

00:03:06.860 --> 00:03:07.740
of his loved ones.

00:03:09.460 --> 00:03:11.040
"'Scuse me, preacher.

00:03:15.400 --> 00:03:16.400
You got time for a sinner?"

00:03:16.820 --> 00:03:20.371
Many of Arvin's actions later in The Devil
All the Time are shocking, but they're all

00:03:20.380 --> 00:03:22.150
set up by this early birthday scene.

00:03:22.320 --> 00:03:23.980
Really, you should've seen 'em all coming.

00:03:25.060 --> 00:03:28.960
All that being said, Arvin Russell isn't like
any character that Holland has played before.

00:03:29.180 --> 00:03:32.900
When he's on screen, Holland is often very
high energy, and he's usually quite chatty.

00:03:33.420 --> 00:03:35.120
"Well I could have stuck the landing a little
better.

00:03:35.130 --> 00:03:36.890
It's just, new suit, well — it's nothing.

00:03:36.890 --> 00:03:38.500
Mr. Stark, it's, it's perfect, thank you.”

00:03:38.640 --> 00:03:40.180
“Yeah we don't really need to start a conversation.”

00:03:40.180 --> 00:03:40.680
“Okay.

00:03:41.100 --> 00:03:42.180
Cap..Captain."

00:03:42.640 --> 00:03:45.780
But unlike so many of Holland's memorable
characters, Arvin doesn't quip.

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:46.540
He doesn't banter.

00:03:46.880 --> 00:03:49.160
In fact, much of the time, he doesn't say
anything at all.

00:03:53.220 --> 00:03:56.260
"Ah, I might've put my foot in something.

00:03:57.040 --> 00:03:58.080
Wasn't my intentions."

00:03:58.660 --> 00:04:00.880
Again, look at the birthday party scene as
an example.

00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:04.020
During the celebration, Holland only has a
few real lines.

00:04:04.470 --> 00:04:08.640
And yet not only does he carry the scene,
but he's able to convey so much crucial information

00:04:08.640 --> 00:04:12.460
about Arvin through his body language alone
that he really doesn't need to speak at all.

00:04:12.890 --> 00:04:16.600
The Devil All the Time is significantly darker
than anything Holland has done before, and

00:04:16.600 --> 00:04:20.660
it directly tackles mature themes that his
previous blockbusters only hinted at.

00:04:21.020 --> 00:04:25.349
It's a challenging movie, and Arvin could
only have been played by a confident, experienced

00:04:25.349 --> 00:04:25.840
actor.

00:04:25.840 --> 00:04:27.560
Naturally, Holland nails it.

